Patch 2153: LD_RING_FENCE_WAIT, live-draw rings that wrap without draining the queue (RSX pass 144, walker plan step 1a).

Today every ring wrap (descriptor, pixel-constant, vertex, constant, upload) calls ld_flush, which submits the open list and
waits for the newest fence: the whole queue drains. With LD_RING_FENCE_WAIT=1 the descriptor rings (SRV tables and
samplers) and the pixel-constant ring wrap differently: ld_submit_nowait sends the open list (a query left open cannot
be split, then the old drain runs), the ring's (range, fence) marks of rsx_ring_marks.h move to the old generation, the
cursor restarts at 0, and every later write into a range first waits for the newest fence that read that range
(ld_rm_ensure; all eight SRV/sampler allocation sites go through ld_srv_take/ld_smp_take, the constant ring waits before
its memcpy). Marks are recorded at both queue submits (flush body, chunk submit), kept per frame slot like the rings, and
cleared wherever the old code reset the cursors (present slot switch, draining flush, movie reset, frame start).
Marks only widen on doubt: overflow merges neighbours with the newest fence, an out-of-order fence or a cursor that moved
back replaces the marks by one mark over the whole range.

Not covered (still drain): the vertex ring (also resets vb/ib/cb and the descriptor rings together), the vertex-constant
ring and the upload ring (ld_flush_body resets it on every flush; many placement sites). Default off (unset or 0 = the old
full drain). One gate line "[ld-ring] fence wait on: ..." when on; [perf-flush] gains "| ringfence desc waits W avoided A
pscb waits W avoided A" in windows where a ring wrapped without a drain (RSX_PERF_W_RFW_* slots).

Host test: tools/smoke/test_ld_ring_marks.sh (mark arithmetic, randomized ring vs a per-slot reference).

